          "text": "# Arkab Posterior: A Quiet Beacon at the Archer\u2019s Heel\n\n*This fourth-magnitude star is a modest naked-eye target\u2014and a reminder that even a familiar point of light is moving through space.*\n\nNot every star in Sagittarius announces itself with the brilliance of a landmark. Arkab Posterior, catalogued as \u03b2\u00b2 Sagittarii, is a subtler prize: a steady point of light near the constellation\u2019s southern reaches, bright enough for unaided eyes under a dark sky but easily lost in a city\u2019s glare. Its name invites a pause. \u201cPosterior\u201d distinguishes it from its neighboring companion in the name pair, Arkab Prior; the star\u2019s catalog identity is HIP 95294. [SIMBAD](https://simbad.cds.unistra.fr/simbad/sim-id?Ident=HIP%2095294) lists its visual magnitude as 4.27.\n\nThat brightness places Arkab Posterior within the usual reach of the naked eye, though visibility depends on sky conditions, eyesight and how high the star stands above the horizon. In a dark location, give your eyes time to adjust and scan Sagittarius rather than expecting this star to leap out. Binoculars can help pick it from the surrounding star field; they will not turn it into a disk. At this distance, even a telescope shows a point of light, not a surface.\n\nThe star\u2019s position is a useful guide for a chart or planetarium app: right ascension 19 hours 23 minutes 13 seconds, declination \u221244 degrees 48 minutes, in coordinates for epoch J2000. Sagittarius is best placed in the evening sky during the Northern Hemisphere\u2019s summer, though how high the constellation climbs depends strongly on the observer\u2019s latitude. From far-northern locations it stays low; farther south, it stands higher. Seek a clear southern horizon, and use a current sky chart to identify the Archer before searching for this fainter target.\n\nThere is more to Arkab Posterior than its appearance suggests. SIMBAD gives a parallax of 23.3612 milliarcseconds. Parall